  "changed_blocks": [
    {
      "path": "established-results.tex",
      "kind": "statements",
      "ordinal": 2,
      "before": "\\begin{proposition}[Execution-time and counter-based separation]\n\\label{prop:counter-separation}\nIn the native executions, computing a KEM after restart, completing\nthe target epoch, and subsequently reopening the journal do not make a\npreviously disclosed reservation an independent secret contribution.\nMoreover, $\\min(c_A,c_B)+1$ is not a universal upper bound on exposed epochs.\n\\end{proposition}",
      "after": "\\begin{proposition}[Execution-time and counter-based separation]\n\\label{prop:counter-separation}\nIn the native executions, computing a KEM after restart, completing\nthe target epoch, and subsequently reopening the journal do not make a\npreviously disclosed reservation an independent secret contribution.\nMoreover, $\\min(c_A,c_B)+1$ is not a universal upper bound on the index of\nan exposed epoch.\n\\end{proposition}"
    },
    {
      "path": "guarded-provenance.tex",
      "kind": "statements",
      "ordinal": 2,
      "before": "\\begin{corollary}[Copies, deletion and late disclosure]\n\\label{cor:guarded-history}\nExposure is monotone under additional acquisitions, direct disclosures\nand key disclosures.  Copying or rewrapping a source adds routes to the\nsame source identity.  Local deletion of a record previously acquired\ndoes not remove its routes from adversarial knowledge.  In particular,\na later matching-key disclosure can expose a source in a ciphertext\nacquired before that disclosure and deleted locally in the meantime.\n\\end{corollary}",
      "after": "\\begin{corollary}[Copies, deletion and late disclosure]\n\\label{cor:guarded-history}\nExposure is monotone under additional acquisitions, direct disclosures\nand key disclosures. Copying or rewrapping a source preserves existing\nroutes and may add routes to the same source identity.\nLocal deletion of a record previously acquired\ndoes not remove its routes from adversarial knowledge.  In particular,\na later matching-key disclosure can expose a source in a ciphertext\nacquired before that disclosure and deleted locally in the meantime.\n\\end{corollary}"
    },
    {
      "path": "guarded-provenance.tex",
      "kind": "proofs",
      "ordinal": 2,
      "before": "\\begin{proof}\nThe cumulative sets and their key closure only grow, and every predicate\nin (GP) is positive.  A source-preserving copy changes a route's record\nidentity or key guards but not its terminal source.  Local deletion\nrestricts future acquisition from that local store; it removes no member\nof $A_t$ and therefore cannot invalidate a satisfied route.\n\\end{proof}",
      "after": "\\begin{proof}\nThe cumulative sets and their key closure only grow, and every predicate\nin (GP) is positive. An exact ciphertext copy reuses its record identity\nand route. Source-preserving rewrapping may change record identities or\nkey guards while retaining the terminal source. Local deletion\nrestricts future acquisition from that local store; it removes no member\nof $A_t$ and therefore cannot invalidate a satisfied route.\n\\end{proof}"
    }
  ],
